  <article class="almanac-prose">
    <section id="two-hosts" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>01.1 · authority profiles</span>
        <h2>The linker is the capability membrane.</h2>
        <p>A Wasm component can call only imports it declares <em>and</em> the host supplies. Dekopon turns that mechanical rule into two reviewable execution products.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="run-mode-cards">
        <article class="run-mode-current">
          <div>{{ status("current", "Current") }}<span><code>dekopon-run</code></span></div>
          <h3>Import-free direct host</h3>
          <p>The linker defines nothing. Components declaring anything except read-only capabilities are rejected before model interaction.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>no WASI, filesystem, network, clock, random, environment, or credentials</li>
            <li>pure provider computation only</li>
            <li>direct success is raw untrusted JSON—not authorization evidence</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="run-mode-future">
          <div>{{ status("current", "Current") }}<span><code>dekopon-brokerd</code></span></div>
          <h3>Broker-owned HTTP host</h3>
          <p>The privileged linker implements exactly <code>dekopon:http@1.0.0</code>. It accepts a component only through a single-use broker authorization.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>no generic WASI, sockets, DNS, files, environment, or raw secrets</li>
            <li>buffered typed requests through a statically linked native engine</li>
            <li>exact policy sits beneath anything guest code requests</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="article-callout callout-decision">
        <strong>An import is not a grant.</strong>
        <p>A provider may structurally require the HTTP interface. It still cannot contact a destination until the broker has authenticated the peer, matched exact policy, and attached narrower per-invocation constraints. An unresolved import fails instantiation; an unauthorized host call fails policy.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="provider-contract" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>01.2 · custom providers</span>
        <h2>Ordinary Rust becomes a typed component.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="build-pipeline">
        <div class="build-step"><span>1</span><strong>Implement</strong><small><code>Provider</code> trait</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div class="build-step"><span>2</span><strong>Generate</strong><small>WIT-facing adapter</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div class="build-step"><span>3</span><strong>Compile</strong><small><code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code></small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div class="build-step build-step-final"><span>4</span><strong>Componentize</strong><small>portable <code>.wasm</code></small></div>
      </div>

      <pre><code><span class="code-purple">pub trait</span> Provider {
    <span class="code-purple">fn</span> describe() -&gt; ProviderManifest;
    <span class="code-purple">fn</span> invoke(
        capability: &amp;CapabilityId,
        input: serde_json::Value,
    ) -&gt; Result&lt;serde_json::Value, ProviderError&gt;;
}</code></pre>

      <p>The stable provider world exports <code>describe</code> and <code>invoke</code>. The SDK adapter parses identifiers and JSON, calls typed Rust, and returns a tagged response. Import-free providers use the published <code>dekopon:provider@0.1.0</code> world directly; HTTP providers compose those exports with the immutable <code>dekopon:http@1.0.0</code> import through caller-generated bindings.</p>

      <div class="invariant-notes">
        <article><span>portable</span><h3>Component artifact</h3><p>The provider ships as Wasm with typed import/export metadata—not as a native dynamic library loaded into the broker.</p></article>
        <article><span>reviewable</span><h3>Import table</h3><p><code>wasm-tools component wit</code> shows which host contracts an artifact requests before it runs.</p></article>
        <article><span>operator-owned</span><h3>Runtime linking</h3><p>No component download can trigger a native plugin download or create a host implementation behind the operator’s back.</p></article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="fresh-store" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>01.3 · per-operation isolation</span>
        <h2>Compile once; start clean every call.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="store-lifecycle">
        <div class="store-shared">
          <span>retained per registry</span>
          <div><strong>Wasmtime Engine</strong><small>runtime configuration</small></div>
          <div><strong>Compiled components</strong><small>machine code amortized</small></div>
          <div><strong>route map</strong><small>capability → provider</small></div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">new operation ↓</i>
        <div class="store-fresh-row">
          <div><span>fresh</span><strong>Store</strong><small>fuel + memory + host state</small></div>
          <div><span>fresh</span><strong>instance</strong><small>new guest state</small></div>
          <div><span>fresh</span><strong>linear memory</strong><small>dies with the call</small></div>
          <div><span>bounded</span><strong>result</strong><small>validated before return</small></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>Sharing an Engine or compiled component does not share a Store. Immediate mode serializes calls behind one runtime mutex; the broker host is asynchronous and can overlap bounded operations, but each invocation still owns a separate Store and authorization-backed host context. Description runs with HTTP disabled, so a component that attempts network activity while describing itself is rejected.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="http-boundary" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>01.4 · constrained host I/O</span>
        <h2>“HTTP access” is never the permission.</h2>
        <p>The useful permission is much smaller: this capability, to this authority, with these methods and ceilings, for this one invocation.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Layer</th><th scope="col">Enforces</th><th scope="col">Cannot be widened by</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Exact policy</th><td>principal, actor, capability, provider, effect metadata</td><td>model text, proposal payload, or provider manifest</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Invocation constraints</th><td>authority + port, methods, host-call count, request/response bytes, timeout</td><td>guest URI, headers, body, or retries</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Native ceilings</th><td>independent upper bounds, HTTPS default, loopback-only plaintext exception</td><td>configuration that asks for more than the host maximum</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Network engine</th><td>DNS validation + pinning, no redirects, no ambient proxy, no decompression</td><td>rebinds, redirect targets, proxy environment, compressed expansion</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Evidence</th><td>method, authorized authority, status, byte counts</td><td>paths, queries, headers, bodies, credentials, raw errors</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>The checked-in JSONPlaceholder provider proves the split with a read-only/idempotent GET capability and a separately named external-write/non-idempotent POST capability. Automated tests use ephemeral loopback servers; they never depend on the public API. The provider may validate its own input, but that validation is never treated as authority.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="limits" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>01.5 · honest boundary</span>
        <h2>Strong isolation, accurately scoped.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="truth-label-cards">
        <article><div>{{ status("current", "Enforced") }}</div><h3>Guest authority</h3><p>Imports, exact policy, fresh stores, fuel, memory, input/output, host calls, network destinations, methods, bytes, and deadlines are bounded in code and tests.</p></article>
        <article><div>{{ status("current", "Separated") }}</div><h3>Process authority</h3><p>The runner never links privileged HTTP or receives authorization. External writes require the separate Unix broker process.</p></article>
        <article><div>{{ status("direction", "Not claimed") }}</div><h3>Host compromise resistance</h3><p>Wasmtime does not protect a compromised broker host, malicious same-UID process, dependency toolchain, or coordinated rollback of both local audit files.</p></article>
      </div>

